-- Q4316: Capital Structure Tax Planning [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 163(a), interest paid on corporate debt is deductible as a business expense, subject to limitations such as the Section 163(j) business interest limitation. This deductibility creates a tax advantage for debt financing over equity financing.
Wrong (A): Interest on legitimate corporate debt is deductible under Section 163(a). Only certain limitations like Section 163(j) may restrict the timing of the deduction.
Wrong (C): Interest payments to bondholders are not dividends. Bondholders receive interest income, which is ordinary income to them, while the corporation claims a deduction under Section 163(a).
Wrong (D): The deductibility of interest under Section 163(a) does not depend on whether the bonds are convertible. Interest on all qualifying corporate debt is deductible regardless of conversion features.$EXPL$
