-- Q4317: Capital Structure Tax Planning [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 301(c), dividends paid by a C corporation are distributions of after-tax earnings and are not deductible by the corporation. This non-deductibility is the fundamental cause of double taxation under the corporate tax structure.
Wrong (A): Dividends are not deductible by the corporation. Unlike interest payments deductible under Section 163(a), dividend distributions provide no tax benefit to the paying corporation.
Wrong (B): Deductibility does not depend on whether dividends are paid from current E&P. Dividends are nondeductible regardless of their source under Section 301(c).
Wrong (D): There is no 50% of taxable income limitation for dividend deductions because dividends are entirely nondeductible. The 50% limitation applies to charitable contributions under Section 170(b), not dividends.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4317;

-- Q4320: Capital Structure Tax Planning [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Sec. 385 and related case law, when shareholder-held debt is reclassified as equity, the interest payments are recharacterized as nondeductible dividend distributions. The corporation loses the interest deduction, and shareholders report the payments as dividends under Sec. 301(c).
Wrong (A): Returns of capital reduce stock basis and are not taxable to the extent of basis under Sec. 301(c)(2). Reclassified interest is treated as dividends from E&P, not as basis recovery.
Wrong (B): The reclassified payments cannot be treated as deductible compensation. The recharacterization under Sec. 385 converts debt to equity, making the payments dividends, not salary.
Wrong (D): Capital gains treatment does not apply to reclassified interest payments. The payments are treated as dividend distributions from E&P under the debt-equity recharacterization rules.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4320;
